{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:scout","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:99874ae2b587f497e69b5f0d31cc638e07bccb8da4817a6cfa2f2e13fc0e8a9e","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.26},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A (OECD-38 GDP-weighted core >= 2.50%/yr avg 2027-2036), marginal 50%. OBSERVED, hard statistical-agency data: US core CPI \"rose to 2.6% in September 2026 from a five-year low of 2.4% in August, slightly exceeding the expected 2.5%\"; euro area core 2.5% in Sep-2026, up from 2.4% in Aug-2026 (Oct-2026 flash due 4 Nov 2026, not yet published — unverified at my horizon). US core PCE: sources conflict — one puts Aug-2026 at 3.1% \"higher than the previous value of 3.0%\", the Sep-2026 print was due 29 Oct 2026 with a 3.1% forecast; I did NOT verify the Sep actual, so treat 3.0-3.1% as the Aug level and the Sep level as unverified. Caveat flagged: one aggregator gave euro area Sep headline HICP at 3.8%, which does not square with 2.5% core and I do not trust it. INFERRED: a GDP-weighted OECD-38 core today is ~2.6-2.8% (US ~half the weight at 2.6-3.1%, euro area 2.5%, Japan ~2%). So the series starts ABOVE the threshold. ARITHMETIC: if 2027-29 averages 2.6%, the 2030-36 average must still be >=2.46% for the 10-yr mean to clear 2.50% — i.e. a persistent ~0.5pp overshoot of target for seven years. Upward: IMF Oct-2026 WEO flags \"energy price pressures\" and global inflation 4.7% in 2026 easing to only 4.1% in 2027 (AE 3.1%); PJM wholesale power +76% in Q1 2026; retail electricity +~7% YoY nationally and +16% YoY in Illinois; LME grade-A copper cash settlement US$14,751/t on 14 Oct 2026 after a record US$14,822 on 12 Oct (single secondary roundup, unconfirmed by a major outlet — treat as approximate); generator step-up transformer lead times \"surpassed 160 weeks by the first quarter of 2026, compared with an average of 143 weeks in 2024\"; gas turbine lead times \"stretched to five years\". Downward: credible central banks, post-capex-bust excess capacity. 50%.\n\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI avg < 1.50%/yr 2027-2036), marginal 58%. OBSERVED, NBS primary release of 14 Oct 2026: September CPI +0.9% y/y and +0.2% m/m, up from 0.8% in August and 0.5% in July; core (ex food and energy) 1.0% y/y, unchanged from August and reported as the first 1.0% in 19 months; PPI +5.0% y/y, accelerating from +3.8% in August. Trading Economics notes the CPI print was \"slightly below market expectations of 1%\". Caveat: one iTiger item reported -0.3% y/y for September but its details match the Oct-2025 release and I discarded it. The reflation evidence (PPI +5.0%, core at 1.0%) is the strongest single argument AGAINST this conjunct and is why I am at 58% not 70%. Against reflation: population -60M over 2026-2035, worker-to-retiree 4.4->2.8, high precautionary saving, pension subsidy burden (locked 2A) — a structurally demand-deficient price level. Recent realized averages: 2022-2026 ~0.4%, 2019-2026 ~1.0%, 2015-2024 ~1.6%. 58%.\n\nCORRELATION: mildly NEGATIVE, ~-0.15. The joint demands a DIVERGENCE, but the live drivers are common-global (energy, copper at records, China PPI +5.0% feeding tradable goods prices) and a China demand deficit exports goods disinflation to the OECD. The offsetting positive-correlation channel is the coherent bifurcation story (OECD labour-scarcity services inflation + Chinese excess capacity). JOINT = 0.50 x 0.58 x 0.90 = 0.261 -> 26.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.122Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Atlanta Fed Wage Growth Tracker, 12-month moving average of median hourly wage growth: